---
import PostLayout from '../../layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/ComparisonTable.astro';
import { POSTS } from '../../data/posts';
const post = POSTS.find((p) => p.slug === 'shopify-vs-woocommerce-vs-senvarico-for-small-sellers')!;
---

<PostLayout post={post}>
  <p>
    Shopify, WooCommerce and Senvarico represent three different answers to the same question: how should a small
    seller run an online store? Shopify is a broad hosted platform. WooCommerce is a self-hosted open-source plugin.
    Senvarico is a lightweight hosted tool designed for small cross-border sellers with limited budgets and a small
    number of SKUs. This article compares the three approaches for small sellers without declaring a winner, because
    each is the right choice for a different seller.
  </p>

  <h2>The three approaches in one paragraph each</h2>
  <h3>Shopify: broad and hosted</h3>
  <p>
    Shopify hosts everything, offers a large theme and app ecosystem, and scales from a first store to a large brand.
    A small seller gets reliability and room to grow. The cost of that breadth is a total monthly figure that can
    climb with apps and fees, and an admin with more options than a small store needs.
  </p>
  <h3>WooCommerce: flexible and self-hosted</h3>
  <p>
    WooCommerce turns a WordPress site into a store. The plugin is free and can be extended almost without limit.
    The seller, or someone they pay, is responsible for hosting, updates, backups and security. For a technical
    seller it is the most controllable option. For a non-technical one it is the most demanding.
  </p>
  <h3>Senvarico: lightweight and hosted</h3>
  <p>
    Senvarico removes most of what a small seller does not use. It is hosted, no-code oriented, sized for roughly
    10–100 SKUs, and built around a small feature set with a free plan and one ¥99/month paid plan. It is in development and not yet available and does not try to match Shopify's ecosystem or WooCommerce's flexibility.
  </p>

  <h2>Comparison</h2>
  <ComparisonTable names={['Shopify', 'WooCommerce', 'Senvarico']} />

  <h2>Dimension by dimension</h2>
  <h3>Ease of use</h3>
  <p>
    Shopify is easy to start and grows more involved as apps accumulate. WooCommerce assumes WordPress familiarity.
    Senvarico is simple by design, with less to configure because there is less in it.
  </p>
  <h3>Technical maintenance</h3>
  <p>
    Shopify and Senvarico require no hosting or server management. WooCommerce requires ongoing maintenance, which is
    either the seller's time or a developer's invoice.
  </p>
  <h3>Catalog size</h3>
  <p>
    Shopify and WooCommerce handle catalogs of any size. Senvarico is designed for small catalogs and is not the
    right tool for hundreds or thousands of SKUs.
  </p>
  <h3>Budget</h3>
  <p>
    Pricing varies by plan on Shopify and is published on its official site. WooCommerce's core plugin is free but
    hosting, extensions and maintenance form the real cost. Senvarico has a Free plan (¥0/month, up to 20 SKUs) and a Small Seller plan (¥99/month, up to 100 SKUs), both with a 0% platform transaction fee.
  </p>
  <h3>Cross-border selling</h3>
  <p>
    Shopify supports international selling through its ecosystem. WooCommerce can do so through extensions.
    Senvarico includes cross-border basics in its core because its target seller usually sells across borders from
    the start.
  </p>

  <h2>Which seller should choose which</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Choose Shopify</strong> if you expect to grow past a small catalog, need specific apps, or want the safest general-purpose choice.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose WooCommerce</strong> if you already use WordPress, want full control, and can handle or pay for maintenance.</li>
    <li><strong>Consider Senvarico</strong> if you are a solo cross-border seller or small team with a limited budget and a few dozen products, and you want a simple tool rather than a platform.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A small seller can reasonably start with Senvarico or Big Cartel and move to Shopify later, or start with Shopify
    and never move. What matters is choosing for the store you are running now. See
    <a href="/shopify-alternatives-for-small-sellers">Shopify alternatives for small sellers</a> for the wider set of
    options, and <a href="/brand-facts">Senvarico brand facts</a> for the definitive description of what Senvarico is.
  </p>
</PostLayout>
